  "body": [
    "WAR IN THE MIDDLE EAST, A BROKEN AGREEMENT IN EUROPE — The economic consequences of the Iran war are no longer limited to rising energy prices and threatened shipping lanes. They are reaching into long-term commercial contracts. On November 10, Polish energy group ORLEN announced that it exercised its right to terminate one of its two long-term LNG supply agreements with QatarEnergy because prolonged force majeure had prevented contractual deliveries.",
    "THE CONTRACT — The oil-linked agreement covered one million metric tonnes of liquefied natural gas annually, equivalent to approximately 15.3 terawatt-hours. ORLEN estimated its value for 2026 at approximately €570 million. Signed in 2009, with deliveries beginning in 2016, it had been scheduled to remain in force until 2034. ORLEN says the contract remains effective for 60 days after the termination notice. The €570 million figure is the estimated 2026 contract value, not an established loss or termination penalty.",
    "WHY QATAR'S EXPORTS WERE DISRUPTED — Reuters reports that Iranian attacks damaged Qatar's Ras Laffan LNG facilities earlier in 2026 and that the conflict severely disrupted shipping through the Strait of Hormuz. QatarEnergy declared force majeure after it could no longer reliably meet some delivery obligations. ORLEN's decision reflects the commercial consequences of prolonged interruptions; it does not establish that QatarEnergy abandoned every customer or stopped all LNG activity.",
    "POLAND HAS NOT ENDED ITS QATAR RELATIONSHIP — ORLEN retains a separate QatarEnergy contract for up to one million tonnes annually through 2034, priced against gas-market benchmarks. The company says its domestic production and other long-term arrangements cover its gas requirements, so ending this agreement does not threaten supply security. That assurance does not independently establish whether replacement supplies are cheaper or more expensive.",
    "WHO BENEFITS FROM THE CHAOS? — Qatar risks losing the stability of an established customer relationship. Poland gains the ability to replace an interrupted oil-linked contract but must manage alternative supplies. Other LNG exporters, including American suppliers, may gain commercial opportunities as buyers diversify. Reuters reports that ORLEN has begun receiving LNG from Venture Global's Louisiana export operations. A shift in contracts is evidence of changing market relationships, not proof that any government intended the conflict to benefit its exporters.",
    "THE BIGGER STRATEGIC QUESTION — Europe's recent energy-security debate centered on reducing reliance on Russian pipeline gas. The disruption to Qatari LNG demonstrates that long-distance imports also face geopolitical risk when production facilities and shipping chokepoints are vulnerable. Diversification can reduce dependence on a single supplier without eliminating exposure to war, freight costs or volatile pricing.",
    "WHAT THE RECORD DOES NOT PROVE — ORLEN's filing confirms the notice, contract terms and its stated rationale. It does not calculate QatarEnergy's final financial losses, prove competitors' net profits, or establish the ultimate cost of replacement cargoes. Nor is there evidence in this transaction that any government deliberately prolonged the war to redistribute LNG business.",
    "THE BOTTOM LINE — A supply relationship expected to last until 2034 is being unwound because the supplier cannot reliably deliver. Poland says it remains supplied; Qatar retains another ORLEN agreement; competitors may gain business. The ongoing EP News investigation asks who gains market share, who loses dependable customers, and who ultimately pays when geopolitical conflict rewrites long-term energy contracts."
  ],
